Looking at this problem, I need to enumerate all circular permutations of 5 labels, run the descending-chain deletion process on each, count total chains $G(S)$, and average.

**Setup.** There are $(5-1)! = 24$ circular permutations. I fix label 1 at position 0 and enumerate all 24 placements of $\{2,3,4,5\}$ in positions 1–4 clockwise. For each cyclic sequence $(a_0,a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4)$ with $a_0=1$, I examine the 5 clockwise steps, identify maximal descending runs (each run of $d$ consecutive descents = one chain of length $d+1$), delete all non-starting points, and repeat on survivors.

**Key mechanics.** A descending chain is a maximal run of consecutive clockwise descents. Its starting point (largest label) survives; all other points in the chain are deleted. Points in ascending runs (not part of any descending chain) also survive. After deletion, survivors retain clockwise order, forming a new circular permutation.

